  "findings": [
    "The six-section constraint merged unrelated algebra, geometry, sheaf, and duality prerequisites into one background chapter.",
    "Definitions of the ordinary and normalized R families were entangled with their recurrence and normalization theorems.",
    "Ordered graphs, compatible sections, graded sheaves, and quotient maps lacked separate definition targets.",
    "Sheaf existence skipped the separate extension, uniqueness, morphism-lifting, splitting, and decomposition nodes.",
    "Word-sheaf construction and flabbiness were conflated with later boundary-sheaf duality and character identities.",
    "Reflection-order existence, unlabelled diamond closure, and transported schedule conventions required explicit entries.",
    "The edge factor, embedded images, scalar quotient, and reverse projectivity were buried behind global comparison statements.",
    "The remaining-condition state and exact R-matrix endpoint identity needed independent nodes to expose the simultaneous induction."
  ],
